WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.087
Download security starts with authorization.

2
00:00:03.087 --> 00:00:13.351
Before returning a protected file, the application should verify that the caller is authenticated when required and allowed to access that specific object.

3
00:00:13.351 --> 00:00:20.914
Ownership, tenant context, sharing permissions, classification, expiration, and workflow state may all matter.

4
00:00:20.914 --> 00:00:27.629
A valid session or API token is not enough if the caller is requesting a file that belongs to someone else.

5
00:00:27.629 --> 00:00:31.796
Indirect references are safer than exposing raw storage paths.

6
00:00:31.796 --> 00:00:41.906
A download URL can reference a server-side file identifier, and the application can map that identifier to trusted storage metadata after authorization.

7
00:00:41.906 --> 00:00:47.771
This reduces the chance that a user can change a path or object key to reach a different file.

8
00:00:47.771 --> 00:00:52.710
It also lets the application log and enforce download policy consistently.

9
00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:59.425
Signed URLs can be useful for time-limited access to a specific resource under controlled conditions.

10
00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:02.897
They are not a replacement for authorization design.

11
00:01:02.897 --> 00:01:12.236
The system should decide who can create a signed URL, how long it lasts, what object it covers, whether it can be reused, and how sharing is logged.

12
00:01:12.236 --> 00:01:17.406
Long-lived or overly broad signed links can become uncontrolled access paths.

13
00:01:17.406 --> 00:01:22.577
Content-Disposition and Content-Type affect how browsers handle downloads.

14
00:01:22.577 --> 00:01:30.140
Some content should be treated as an attachment rather than displayed inline, especially when it is user-supplied.

15
00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:39.247
Safe content type handling reduces the chance that a browser interprets untrusted content as active HTML, script, or another risky format.

16
00:01:39.247 --> 00:01:48.894
Export features should also be reviewed for excessive data exposure because generated files often aggregate more fields than normal screens show.
